    https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/

  spice/naif0012.tls
    NASA NAIF leap-second kernel. See "NAIF SPICE kernels" below.


DATA DOWNLOADED BY tools/fetch_data.py (not in this repository)

  NAIF SPICE kernels: de440s, mar099, jup365, sat441, nep097, plu060
    NASA/JPL Navigation and Ancillary Information Facility.
    LICENSE NOT STATED FOR THE KERNELS. NAIF's rules page sets out a "SPICE
    Software License" and encourages acknowledgment of NAIF and of the teams that
    made the kernels; it does not name a license for kernel data.
    https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/naif/rules.html
    Pantheon reads kernels with its own reader and includes no SPICE software.

  JPL Horizons vector tables (assets/data/spacecraft)
    NASA/JPL Solar System Dynamics. Citation: Giorgini et al., JPL Horizons.
    LICENSE NOT FOUND. No license or terms appear on the Horizons page as fetched:
    https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/
    That page relies on JavaScript, so this is what was not found, not proof that
    none exists.

  Planet, Sun and ring maps (assets/data/textures)
    Solar System Scope, https://www.solarsystemscope.com/textures/
    "Distributed under Attribution 4.0 International license" (CC BY 4.0).
    Credit: Solar System Scope. The publisher states the maps are based on NASA
    elevation and imagery data with colours tuned and slightly saturated, and that
    unmapped gaps are filled with fictional terrain.

  Milky Way panorama (assets/data/textures/eso_milkyway.jpg)
    eso0932a, https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso0932a/
    Credit: ESO/S. Brunier. CC BY 4.0 (https://www.eso.org/public/outreach/copyright/).
    ESO requires the credit to be clear and visible; the app shows it on screen.


COMPILED TABLES (embedded in src/data.rs and src/sim.rs)

  Named stars, Local Group galaxies, galaxy clusters, moon elements, planet
  elements, and IAU rotation elements are facts compiled from the literature and
  from JPL's public tables, as cited in src/evidence.rs. Sizes are approximate.


VENDORED CODE

  three.js r180 with its post-processing addons (pantheon-web/lib), MIT License,
  Copyright 2010-2025 Three.js Authors. Pantheon 3D's window uses Tauri (MIT or
  Apache-2.0, downloaded by Cargo) and links the WebKitGTK your system provides
  (LGPL, not included here).

  Rust dependencies are downloaded by Cargo and keep their own licenses, listed in
  each crate's metadata. Cargo.lock records the exact versions.

  The bloom in pantheon-web/js/post.js follows Jimenez, "Next Generation Post
  Processing in Call of Duty: Advanced Warfare", SIGGRAPH 2014, and its tone curve is
  Stephen Hill's fit of ACES. The latitude-longitude lookup in
  pantheon-web/js/scene/shaders.js uses Tarini's seam fix (JGT 2012). The atmosphere
  there is a textbook single-scattering integral with published optical depths.

  The AgX tonemap in shaders/tonemap.wgsl follows the polynomial fit published by
  Benjamin Wrensch. The zoom-and-pan flight path in src/frames.rs follows van Wijk
  and Nuij, "Smooth and efficient zooming and panning", IEEE InfoVis 2003.
